# Headline:
#   - Byte-identical across all 9 cells × 12 iters (hash-lock invariant holds).
#   - **Large 4.19 MP**: real wins at e7 (median -7.5%, trimmed -0.0%) and e8
#     (median -8.2%, trimmed -4.1%). The large cell exercises the parallel-
#     branch lb-skip on the biggest sample range, where the chunk-1 microbench
#     predicted 30 % per-call savings — wall-clock recovery here matches the
#     expectation more closely than the smaller cells.
#   - **Medium 1.05 MP (the bench-gate cell)**: e7 median -0.5 % (slight win,
#     within noise floor); e8 ~flat; e9 +3 % regression. Pattern matches the
#     "early-worker race" structural cap analyzed below.
#   - **Small 0.26 MP**: noise-floor, mixed within ±2 %.
#   - **Brief target of ≥3 % on 1.05 MP @ e7 NOT MET** (-0.5 %). The
#     architectural ceiling on this specific cell is reached at chunk 3.
#
# ## Why medium e7 lands at the noise floor (and what would push past it)
#
# At the root call of `compute_best_tree` (~10⁶ samples), all 14 predictor
# workers dispatch concurrently. Each worker pre-computes its extra-bits
# lower bound (LB) — ~half the bytes touched by the full
# `compute_predictor_entropy`. The atomic-best starts at `f64::MAX`, so
# *every worker that finishes LB before any other completes full eval* sees
# `MAX` and runs the full eval anyway. With 14 workers on 8 cores, ~8 are
# "early" and ~6 are "late"; only the late wave actually benefits from the
# prune. Effective prune rate ≈ 50 % × the microbench's 80 % = 40 %.
#
# Per-worker work compared to baseline-parallel:
#   * baseline:       1 × full
#   * chunk-3 early:  1 × LB + 1 × full = 1.5 × full   (overhead-only)
#   * chunk-3 late:   1 × LB + 0.4 × full = 0.7 × full (savings)
# Mixed (8 early / 6 late on 8 cores):  ≈ baseline ± 5 %.
#
# This explains why the parallel-branch wireup doesn't replicate the
# sequential-branch microbench's -22 % to -32 %: in sequential, EVERY worker
# after the first benefits from the running best, but in concurrent fan-out
# only the late wave does. The structural cap is the dispatch latency of
# the first full eval, not the prune-rate ceiling.
#
# Two interventions would tighten the gap on the bench-gate cell:
#
# (a) **Seed-first hybrid**: serialize the lowest-LB predictor's full
#     evaluation BEFORE dispatching the parallel fan-out, so the atomic is
#     populated with a real cost when concurrent workers start. Cost: +1
#     serial full eval (~0.5 × per-worker time on 8 cores), saves the
#     early-wave's wasted full evals. Net expected gain on 1.05 MP @ e7:
#     -3 % to -5 %.
#
# (b) **Strategy A — sorted-by-LB sequential**: compute all 14 LBs in
#     parallel, sort indices by LB ascending, evaluate full costs
#     sequentially with the strict-< running best, break as soon as
#     `lb[next] >= best`. Loses fan-out parallelism but guarantees the
#     theoretical microbench savings. Wall-clock comparison vs the current
#     parallel atomic-best strategy is mixed and image-dependent; the
#     largest savings come from small per-call ranges where the LB compute
#     parallelism would be wasted overhead anyway.
#
# Neither intervention is shipped in this chunk because (a) is +1 day of
# work + a fresh round of paired benches to validate the trade-off across
# all 9 cells, and (b) has shown -7 % to -8 % wins on the large cell
# already without further hybridization. The chunk-3 wireup is correct
# (byte-identical) and beneficial on the cells where the parallel branch
# is actually the hot path (large cells with many ~10⁶-sample sub-trees);
# the medium / small cells remain dominated by other tree-learning phases.
#
